== behaviorism vs reductionism ==
[[wn.RLN:quines_errors#behaviourism]]
difference between the behaviourist theory of content and behaviourist theory of
justification exactly parallels distinction between empiricist theory of content
and empiricist theory of justification.

== three-term contingency ==
see: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three-term_contingency
1) Antecedent stimulus (a signal that the reinforcement/punishment is available)
2) Behaviour (the behaviour that is conditioned)
3) Reinforcement/behaviour

This becomes momentous/significant when combined with the doctrine:
“all instrumental/operant behaviour including verbal behaviour, both that of
the speaker and that of listener, is acquired, maintained and abandoned in
accordance with the principle of the three term contingency (Skinner 1969)”
from [[local:place1998-behaviourismAsAStandpointInLinguistics.pdf]]

The inadequate complexity of all three elements is where we can invoke much the
same argument as for [[wn.notes:confirmation_holism]]

non-operant behaviour is:
reflexive (automatically elicited by sitmulus)
classically conditioned (Pavlovian) not dependent upon reinforcement
elicited rather than emitted
most importantly – controlled by antecedent stimuli not by consequences


